# Binary addition. Two numbers, most significant bit first.
#
#   rw progs/binadd.rw '<1011+110>'   ->   10001
#
# Addition wants to start at the least significant end, but the two operands
# end in different places -- one before the "+", one before the ">". So each
# column is run by taking the last bit of the right operand, turning it into a
# traveller (T carries a 0, U carries a 1), and walking it left to the carry
# marker, which sits where the "+" was and remembers the carry in its own
# identity: P for no carry, Q for a carry.
#
# The marker is also where the answer is built. Each column's result bit is
# deposited immediately to its right, and since columns arrive least
# significant first, each new bit lands to the left of the previous one -- the
# answer assembles itself most significant first, for free.
#
#   < left operand | P | answer | separator | right operand | >
#
# The separator keeps the launch from eating the answer once the right operand
# runs out, and the traveller carries it along as it passes.

# bootstrap: the "+" becomes the carry marker, and the answer starts empty
+->P|

# a traveller reaches the marker: one column of the addition.
# left bit, carried bit and carry in; result bit and carry out.
0PT->P0
1PT->P1
0PU->P1
1PU->Q0
0QT->P1
1QT->Q0
0QU->Q0
1QU->Q1

# the left operand is spent, so its bit is a 0
<PT-><P0
<PU-><P1
<QT-><P1
<QU-><Q0

# a traveller on its way left, over the answer and over the separator
0T->T0
1T->T1
|T->T|
0U->U0
1U->U1
|U->U|

# launch: take the last bit of the right operand. Above everything below it,
# so nothing further down can run while the right operand still has bits.
0>->T>
1>->U>

# right operand spent: ripple the carry through what is left of the left one,
# then hand its remaining bits straight to the answer
0Q->P1
1Q->Q0
0P->P0
1P->P1

# both spent. A carry out of the top becomes the new leading 1.
<Q-><P1

# drop leading zeros, but only while two digits remain
P00->P0
P01->P1

# scaffolding off
<P->
|>->
